**Current support versus accepted design.** The Alpha builder still treats authored runtime work and fragments as HTML entry activity. The accepted queued design requires an explicit root purpose. Initially, only root-only `$page` supplies the HTML purpose. Dependency roots remain non-activating in both designs.

Every selected normal module is compiled into an immutable module artefact. Its top-level runtime work becomes a dormant compiler-synthesised `start`. Entry assembly activates that module-owned `start` exactly once for a selected page. A dependency exposes declarations without activating its root.

### Explicit root purpose

Top-level executable work or direct output requires a recognised purpose with an
applicable consumer. `$page` is the initial and only HTML purpose in the
accepted design:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page
]

The purpose applies to the whole normal root rather than to statements after
the directive. A root with only functions, types, constants or other API
declarations remains valid without a page. An unmarked root with authored
runtime work, direct output or fragments still needs a purpose even when an
optimisation could prove the work unreachable. A static `if false`, an empty
direct fragment and an immutable runtime binding do not erase the authored
role.

Future `$test` may provide a non-page purpose for a separate testing consumer.
It is deferred and cannot make a root valid today. A metadata directive also
cannot authorise runtime work merely because it appears in the file.

### Comment and documentation exemptions

Semantically classified `$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates are
documentation/comment processing. They do not need a root purpose and do not
create an HTML entry by themselves. This exemption follows the template's
semantic category, not whether its rendered output happens to be empty.

Assigned or returned template values are ordinary values, not direct output.
Their containing runtime code follows the normal purpose rule.

### Entry-activated root runtime

The selected module root's top-level runtime code becomes a dormant
compiler-synthesised `start` function. Entry assembly activates it exactly
once. This includes top-level bindings, function calls and other runtime
statements. Helper files define reusable declarations. They do not own an
export block, an independent `start` or an entry.

### Page fragments

Two forms of direct top-level template in a module root become page fragments:

- A const fragment uses the `#` prefix before the opening bracket. It folds at compile time and is emitted as static HTML.
- A runtime fragment uses the plain bracket form without `#`. It is evaluated by the module's `start` function when entry assembly activates it and inserted into the page at runtime.

The builder preserves source order between static and runtime fragments. Static
fragments are emitted immediately. Runtime fragments use generated slots in the
HTML document, then the embedded JavaScript calls the module start function and
fills those slots.

### Metadata-only pages

A root with only `$page` metadata still emits a complete page with default
document metadata. A page containing only resource-bearing metadata also emits
the complete document and the resources it uses. It does not need non-empty
executable HIR, an artificial fragment or generated runtime code.

### Dependency roots

Dependency module roots never activate their root runtime. When a module is
bound for its public API, its top-level runtime code, `start` body and page
fragments remain dormant. Only exported declarations remain available.

The purpose policy still applies when a root is compiled as a provider. A
consumer cannot make invalid unpurposed runtime work legal by keeping it
dormant. Valid dependency access and `check` remain non-activating, but neither
operation suppresses a purpose diagnostic.

### Do not put page fragments in helper files

Helper files should define reusable declarations. The module root should bind
and use them. Page-producing top-level templates belong in the selected module
root only. An ordinary file cannot become a page by containing a fragment.

### Related concepts

- For module identity and root filenames see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For page metadata and command policy see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and page directives).
- For routes and synthetic single-file pages see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
